> Continuing my goal from [January](2026-jan), here's a bunch of shit a random guy on the
> internet did this month.

# Stuff I Did
## Ran
I wrote at the end of January that I wanted to run 40km per week, and I did not come
anywhere close to this sadly. There are a variety of excuses I could make for this, but to
be honest I think it was a little overambitious anyway coming off of a November/December
where I ran very little. I at least upped my distance (very slightly), so I'm happy with
that:

* I ran a total of 80km, up only 6.5 (so, one run) from January.
* This was a total time of 6:42:00 on feet, with my average speed at 12.12 km/hr (about
4:57 per km), which is a must faster pace than last month, so that's encouraging.
* I'm not even going to bother to list my total elevation, because it's basically a
rounding error.

## Played
In contrast to January, I did not slam back giant narrative games like there was no
tomorrow (perhaps a bit of fatigue there, combined with having a lot more to do this
month), but I did play a much wider variety of stuff:
* [Q-UP](https://store.steampowered.com/app/3730790/QUP/), which is pretty perfectly done
satire, especially as someone who was heavily involved in the esports scene for League of
Legends during college. It also just tickles my brain, because it's one giant optimization
problem.
* I finally finished the [Baldur's Gate 3](https://store.steampowered.com/app/1086940/Baldurs_Gate_3/)
Dark Urge playthrough that a close friend and I started over a year ago. I'm happy to say
that Durge is just as fun as a normal playthrough, and being comically over the top evil
never gets old.
* [Super Battle Golf](https://store.steampowered.com/app/4069520/Super_Battle_Golf/) was
one I picked up with a few friends because it looked like a blast, and I'm happy to say
that it very much is. I can't remember the last time I laughed hard enough to cry and then
cursed out my best friends within the span of 30 seconds. It's the quintessential
definition of "does precisely one thing to perfection" for me.
* I made the mistake of starting a game of [Civilization VI](https://store.steampowered.com/app/289070/Sid_Meiers_Civilization_VI/)
on a whim one morning, only to discover 2 days later that I had not gone outside (as one
does with Civilization). It had been a long time since I played, but I still cruised
pretty comfortably to a diplomatic victory as the Netherlands (felt appropriate) and had a
great time escaping the gray outside ~~while reducing my enemies to rubble so that fewer
people could vote against me in the world congress~~.
* I've been playing a [Daggerheart](https://www.daggerheart.com/) campaign with my normal
D&D group while we take a break from my main campaign. I'm a player this time (usually I'm
the forever DM) in a Western themed magic setting, and I have to say that Daggerheart is
pretty awesome. It basically nails exactly the narrative-forward style of TTRPGs that I
like, and I'm having a blast playing as Jessie, the mayor's daughter who just wants to get
into bar fights and carries a double barrel shotgun disguised as a parasol.

## Read
### Books
I finished 7 books in February (though 2 of them were more like novellas and one of them I
had already begun months ago):
* [The Shadow of What Was Lost](https://hardcover.app/books/the-shadow-of-what-was-lost) - ehhhh. 600 pages of setup and then speedruns the climax
only to set up even more confusing stuff. It wasn't *bad*, just a little too grandiose for
me (though I guess I shouldn't be surprised, Islington's Heirarchy series is very similar
even though I like it a lot more).
* [Golden Son](https://hardcover.app/books/golden-son) - better than Red Rising for me,
really hard to put down, but not quite an all-timer. I was hesitant to return to the Red
Rising universe after the first book's absolute brutality, but I'm glad I did, because
the first book is by far the weakest.
* [Morning Star](https://hardcover.app/books/morning-star) - Also very good and a great
ending to the original Red Rising trilogy. I'm happy I stuck with it because the books are
just page turners start to finish, and I already have the sequel trilogy lined up for
March and April.
* I finally finished *Harry Potter en de Steen Der Wijzen* (the Dutch translation of the
Sorcerer's Stone (yes I'm using the American title, sue me)). The language comprehension
has really started to pick up for me, which feels awesome. I'm already halfway through
*Harry Potter en de Geheime Kamer* and can do entire chapters in one sitting. Also side
note, the title of this one in Dutch annoys me to no end (literally "The Secret Room"
rather than "The Chamber **OF** Secrects").
* I read [The Slow Regard of Silent Things](https://hardcover.app/books/the-slow-regard-of-silent-things)
and [The Narrow Road Between Desires](https://hardcover.app/books/the-narrow-road-between-desires) after finding them
at a local bookshop and feeling a hankering for some Rothfuss while I wait for the final
book in the Kingkiller trilogy that will never come out. Both are very good, though *The
Slow Regard of Silent Things* in particular is a very odd read just in general, but
especially if you haven't read the main books.
* Finally, I read [Coconut](https://hardcover.app/books/coconut-346124c0-7bdd-4efc-8def-5aec969e6caf)
after getting it in a "blind date with a book" package from the same bookshop. It's a
lovely (and slightly harrowing) read, and having a memoir was a great palette cleanser
after all the grand fantasy/sci-fi. Recommend!

### Not Books
A few more things in this category this month, but here's some associated links I found
interesting:
* [*Don't Get Distracted*](https://calebhearth.com/dont-get-distracted), a talk about how
engineers can often miss the larger picture when working with an interesting problem.
Slightly disturbing to read, but really important (and a big reason I flat refuse to work
for anything military or military-adjacent).
* I reread [this really great article](https://lux-magazine.com/article/privacy-eroticism/)
on how the normalization of a lack of privacy has killed a lot of eroticism. I promise
it's not a super sexual article other than passing mentions of things, but anecdotally I found
it resonating a lot with my own experience, particularly this bit:

> Even when, as in my experience in the salon, I am acting in the privacy of my own body,
> someone is always there watching, ready to interpret my actions, problematize them so as
> to share in the same sense of magical thinking, the same insecurities, and to be
> punished for not being insecure in the same way.

* I went down [Dave Gauer's blog](https://ratfactor.com) as a bit of a rabbit hole after
finding one of his articles on [lobste.rs](https://lobste.rs). A lot of his writing as an engineer
resonates with me, in particular [*I'm an American software developer and the "broligarchs" don't speak for me*](https://ratfactor.com/tech-nope).
* On a lighter note to end this section, [*How far back in time can you understand English?*](https://www.deadlanguagesociety.com/p/how-far-back-in-time-understand-english)
was fascinationg. It started to read pretty close to Dutch around 1400 for me, and I was
completely lost at 1100. As a great follow-up, [this video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=842OX2_vCic)
that does the same thing but spoken aloud is also super interesting.


