## Made
### Code
* At the start of the month I hammered out [Doctor Wondertainment](https://codeberg.org/garrettm/doctor-wondertainment)
in a few hours. It's just a super simple Discord bot that my friends and I use to keep a
database of things we recommend to each other.
* I swapped my desktop to daily drive [Niri](https://github.com/YaLTeR/niri) as my
compositor after a couple false starts, and after using it for about two weeks now without
swapping back to Hyprland I am very satisfied. The "infinite strip workspace" model works
really well for me in terms of compartmentalizing different things I'm doing on my
computer at once.
* As always, I tinkered and tweaked my dotfiles because I can't leave a good thing well
enough alone. Other than adding niri as mentioned above, the only major change this month
was swapping no a new color scheme ([`nordic`](https://github.com/AlexvZyl/nordic.nvim)),
which I also enjoyed forking and tweaking myself.
* I mentioned last month that I had an application I'd wanted to build for a while, and I
started that project this month titled [Thoth](https://codeberg.org/garrettm/thoth). Right
now it's just a personal strava stats bakcup and aggregator, but I intend it to be a sort
of "personal timeline" thing as it grows. It's also my first forray into a larger piece of
software written entirely in `zig`, which I'm having a great time with.

### Not Code
* I said in January that I wanted to make one completely new dish every week from a random
country. Sadly, my evenings were super busy this month, but I did manage at least two:
    * Kosovo, where I chose to make stuffed peppers seeing as there were not a ton of
    interesting options (at least for me). They were fine. They tasted like stuffed
    peppers.
    * Bhutan, where I chose to make [Ema datshi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ema_datshi).
    This one was much better (who'd have thought that  fried peppers in a cheese stew was
    tasty), and I plan to make it again with more interesting pepper varieties.
* I also perfected my coffee brewing in February, because I am a caricature of a Software
Engineer: split keyboard, spends way too much time on coffee, uses weird tiny languages
for fun. Even though I'm in Europe now I still am heavily predisposed to American
diner-style pour-over coffee, so now I make that every morning (but with good beans and a
hand grinder because I'm insufferable).

## Watched
There's only one thing in this category, which is [this video from Jeff Pelletier](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S774m29AYr4)
about racing the Badwater 135 mile ultramarathon in Death Valley. I really like Jeff's
stuff and I've got running an ultra on my personal bucket list in the next 5 years (though
I'm looking at 100k, not 100+ miles), so it's fun to see what basically amounts to the
most evil possible race someone could concoct (barring the Barkleys). Especially
interesting to me in this was how the intense focus on health statistics and monitoring
basically didn't matter in the end (or at least didn't have as large of an impact as
intended) becuase it's pushing the body so far outside the norm that you just can't
account for everything.

# Stuff I Didn't Do
This month there wasn't much I completely missed out on, mostly just a lot of stuff that I
only half-finished:
* As mentioned at the very start, I was aiming to run 40km a week. I did not hit that,
*but* I do feel like I'm getting much faster, and I'm in some of the best shape of my life
already.
* Similarly, I wanted to cook one new thing a week, but only managed that twice. Largely
this was because I spent a lot of time with friends in the evenings though, and in a
couple instances eating new things with them, so half points.
* I said in January that I had a short story kicking around in my head that I wanted to
write the draft of. I did not finish the draft, but I have at least begun one and have the
broad strokes, which is better than nothing.

# Stuff I'm gonna do in March
This go around with the benefit of two months of hindsight I'm going to try and reel
myself back to some less ambitious targets:
* Run my 10k PR at the end of the month. I have a small local race I'm doing with friends
on the last weekend of March, and I think it's a good opportunity to try for it (and smoke
them all in the process). This feels a lot better to me than a weekly distance goal,
though I want to slowly ramp that back up as well.
* Finish the draft of my short story. This is very achievable, I just need to accept that
some of the dialogue isn't as perfect as I want and get some actual feedback on it.
* Though I plan to keep tinkering on `thoth`, I've also had what I think is a pretty neat
game idea that I've been writing out the design doc for over the last few days. It's rare
that I can see the "finished state" of a project so clearly in my head off the rip, so
this feels like one I'd really like to pursue. I'm trying to be realistic and don't plan
to have a fully playable thing at all this month, but I'd like to at least get something
super barebones to playtest with my friends.


If you've made it all the way to the end of my ramblings, congratulations! You've earned
these two sentences, which exist because I hate ending things on bullet point lists.
